course: dictionary
lesson: 1741
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word clap can mean hitting your hands together, or a loud sharp sound. To find the right meaning, read the words around it: they are its context.
explain: «At a concert, play or assembly you stay quiet during the show and clap at the end.» Here clap means hitting your hands together to show you enjoyed the show. The words concert and show are the clues.
explain: «If I stamped with my foot, the palace would vanish in a clap of thunder.» Here a clap is one loud, sharp sound. Thunder is the clue. Always ask: which meaning fits here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Clap means to hit your hands together, usually again and again, to show you like something or to keep a beat.
fact: A clap is one loud, sharp sound, like «a clap of thunder».
fact: Clap can also mean to put something somewhere quickly and firmly, as in «clap a hand over your mouth».
fact: «Clap eyes on» means to see something.
fact: Clapping the syllables of a word helps you count them: but-ter-fly has 3 claps.
exercise: «At the end of the show, the whole audience began to clap.» What does clap mean here? | See something for the first time | Put something quickly and firmly somewhere | One loud, sharp sound | *Hit your hands together to show you liked it
exercise: «The sky lit up, and a clap of thunder shook the windows.» What is a clap in this sentence? | A gentle whisper | A round of applause | *One loud, sharp sound | A quick, firm push
exercise: Your teacher says, «Clap the word but-ter-fly.» How many claps will you make? | *3 | 5 | 4 | 2
test: «Mia clapped her hands in time with the music to keep the beat.» What does clapped mean here? | One loud sharp sound | Put her hands quickly over her face | *Hit her hands together to keep a beat | Stopped her hands moving
test: Tom asks, «Did you clap eyes on my blue pencil case?» What does clap eyes on mean? | *See | Hear | Throw | Hit
exam: Why does Ella clap her hands along with the drum? | *To keep the beat | To see the drum | To hide her hands | To count the rain
